\begin_layout Section
The Stabilizer Code 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Vibe
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:The-Stabilizer-Code"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{X}:=\hat{\sigma}_{x}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{Z}:=\hat{\sigma}_{z}$
\end_inset

.
 Errors in QC occur from 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

random and unpredictable [appearances] of 
\begin_inset Formula $\X$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\Z$
\end_inset

 during the evaluation of a computation.
 They can be detected by repeated measurements of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{X}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{Z}$
\end_inset

 but since 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[\hat{X},\hat{Z}\right]\neq0$
\end_inset

, such repeated measurements (by causing projections on to the respective
 eigenstates) destroy the quantum state.
 For example, a 
\begin_inset Formula $\Z$
\end_inset

 measurement 
\begin_inset Formula $M_{\Z}$
\end_inset

 on a qubit with state 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi$
\end_inset

 (randomly) will project that qubit onto 
\begin_inset Formula $\0$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $\1$
\end_inset

 with corresponding (measured) eigenvalues 
\begin_inset Formula $+1$
\end_inset

 or -1 (respectively), leading to a complete loss of the state's amplitude
 and phase
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Wave function collapse
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 A subsequent measurement 
\begin_inset Formula $M_{\X}$
\end_inset

 of 
\begin_inset Formula $\X$
\end_inset

 will then project onto the eigenstate 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\0+\1\right)/\sqrt{2}$
\end_inset

 or the eigenstate 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\0-\1\right)/\sqrt{2}$
\end_inset

 (with corresponding measured eigenvalues 
\begin_inset Formula $+1$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $-1$
\end_inset

).
 Call this the 
\series bold
projective measurement problem
\series default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The projective measurement problem can be avoided by measuring multiple
 qubits; consider a two qubit system, with qubits 
\begin_inset Formula $a,b$
\end_inset

, and the standard 
\begin_inset Formula $\X_{i},\Z_{i}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 indicates which qubit the operator will act on.
 Construct the two-qubit operators 
\begin_inset Formula $\X_{a}\X_{b}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Z_{a}\Z_{b}$
\end_inset

; these operators commute since 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[\X_{a},\Z_{b}\right]=0$
\end_inset

 (since they're applied to different qubits).
 Thus, these operators can be measured simultaneously
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
If operators 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

 commute, then you can come up with a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

complete
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 set of basis states 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{f_{n}g_{n}}$
\end_inset

 that are eigenstates of both 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

, and thus express any state of the quantum system as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\ket{\Psi}=\sum_{n}\alpha_{n}\ket{f_{n}g_{n}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Measuring 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 you will get eigenstate 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{m}$
\end_inset

 with probability 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\alpha_{m}^{2}\right|$
\end_inset

 while leaving the system in eigenstate 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{f_{m}g_{m}}$
\end_inset

, and vice-versa for 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

 (i.e., you get the same result either way).
 Thus, it doesn't matter which order you measure 
\begin_inset Formula $F,G$
\end_inset

 in, you will get a consistent result.
 This is the significance of commuting operators.
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 The possible outcomes of a measurement of both 
\begin_inset Formula $\X_{a}\X_{b}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Z_{a}\Z_{b}$
\end_inset

 appear in table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eigenstates-two-ops"

\end_inset

.
 
