\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Eigenstates of the two-qubit operators 
\begin_inset Formula $\X_{a}\X_{b}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Z_{a}\Z_{b}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "eigenstates-two-ops"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consider the state 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi=\left(\ket{00}+\ket{11}\right)/\sqrt{2}$
\end_inset

 and notice that an 
\begin_inset Formula $\X_{b}$
\end_inset

 error (i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\X$
\end_inset

 error on the second qubit) will transform 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi$
\end_inset

 into 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\ket{01}+\ket{10}\right)/\sqrt{2}$
\end_inset

 
\emph on
which is also an eigenstate of 
\begin_inset Formula $\X_{a}\X_{b}\Z_{a}\Z_{b}$
\end_inset

 
\emph default
but with eigenvalues 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(-1,+1\right)$
\end_inset

.
 That change - measurements
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Don't forget: we only know anything about 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi$
\end_inset

 through measurements.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 of 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi$
\end_inset

 (by giving 
\emph on

\begin_inset Formula $\X_{a}\X_{b}\Z_{a}\Z_{b}$
\end_inset


\emph default
) giving eigenvalues 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(-1,+1\right)$
\end_inset

 instead of 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(+1,+1\right)$
\end_inset

 - reveals the error 
\emph on
without disturbing the wave function 
\emph default
(amplitude and phase) of 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi$
\end_inset

.
 Unfortunately, this error cannot be distinguished from a 
\begin_inset Formula $\X_{a}$
\end_inset

, which yields the same final state and eigenvalues.
 The point here is that 
\series bold
while errors can always be detected, they cannot always be uniquely identified
 using only 
\series default

\begin_inset Formula $\X_{a}\X_{b}$
\end_inset

 
\series bold
and 
\series default

\begin_inset Formula $\Z_{a}\Z_{b}$
\end_inset

.
 A more complex scheme is necessary.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The operators 
\begin_inset Formula $\X_{a}\X_{b}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Z_{a}\Z_{b}$
\end_inset

 are called 
\series bold
stabilizers
\series default
.

\series bold
 
\series default
The core idea of stabilizer codes is repeated measurements of a complete
 set of commuting stabilizers
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Don't get hung up on complete and commuting - we'll come back to it.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 puts a qubit system into a simultaneous and unique eigenstate of all of
 them
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Think about how solving a set of simultaneous equations gives you a unique
 solution.
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 Since you can measure (the observables corresponding to) the stabilizers
 without perturbing the system
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
That's where the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

stable
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 in stabilizers comes from.
\end_layout

\end_inset

, you can catch (some) errors when they occur (evident by transitions from
 one eigenstate of the stabilizer to another).
 When stabilizer codes are used, the system is put into a random starting
 eigenstate of the stabilizers, a so-called 
\series bold
quiescent state
\series default
 of the system, by simultaneously measuring all stabilizers
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Thus, the system doesn't necessarily start from the groundstate, at the
 beginning of the quantum computation/quantum circuit.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 and then measured repeatedly at regular intervals.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
The Surface Code
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
More complex stabilizer codes exist and are used; the following is a description
 of such a code, called a 
\series bold
surface code
\series default
.
 A surface code can be implemented using a two-dimensional array of real
 (physical) qubits, as shown in figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "2dlattice-1-1"

\end_inset

.
