\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
(a) A two-dimensional array implementing a surface code.
 
\series bold
Data qubits
\series default
 are open circles (
\begin_inset Formula $\bigcirc$
\end_inset

), 
\series bold
measurement qubits
\series default
 are filled circles (
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
like{10}
\end_layout

\end_inset

), with measure-
\begin_inset Formula $\Z$
\end_inset

 qubits colored green and measure-
\begin_inset Formula $\X$
\end_inset

 qubits colored yellow.
 (b) Neighbor measurement sequence; note that the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

zig-zagging
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 is important.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset

The physical qubits are one of two types: 
\series bold
data qubits
\series default
, within which the computational quantum states are stored, and 
\series bold
measurement qubits
\series default
, which are used as stabilizers, but can also alter the state of the data
 qubits, during, e.g., error correction.
 There are two types of measurement qubits: 
\series bold
measure-
\begin_inset Formula $\Z$
\end_inset

 
\series default
qubits (measuring the 
\begin_inset Formula $\Z$
\end_inset

 syndrome) and 
\series bold
measure-
\begin_inset Formula $\X$
\end_inset

 
\series default
qubits (measuring the 
\begin_inset Formula $\X$
\end_inset

 syndrome).
 Each data qubit is connected to two of each kind of measurement qubit (two
 measure-
\series bold

\begin_inset Formula $\Z$
\end_inset

 
\series default
qubits and two measure-
\begin_inset Formula $\X$
\end_inset

 qubits).
 Conversely, each measurement qubit is connected to four data qubits.
 
\series bold
Very important
\series default
: measure-
\series bold

\begin_inset Formula $\Z$
\end_inset

 
\series default
qubit triggers a 
\begin_inset Formula $\Z$
\end_inset

 measurement on each of its neighboring data qubits (
\begin_inset Formula $\Z_{a}\Z_{b}\Z_{c}\Z_{d}$
\end_inset

 on its 
\begin_inset Formula $a,b,c,d$
\end_inset

 neighbors) and likewise a measure-
\series bold

\begin_inset Formula $\X$
\end_inset

 
\series default
qubit triggers a 
\begin_inset Formula $\X$
\end_inset

 measurement on each of its neighboring data qubits (see figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "2dlattice-1-2"

\end_inset

).
 Therefore, a measure-
\series bold

\begin_inset Formula $\Z$
\end_inset

 
\series default
measures the stabilizer 
\begin_inset Formula $\Z_{a}\Z_{b}\Z_{c}\Z_{d}$
\end_inset

 and measure-
\series bold

\begin_inset Formula $\X$
\end_inset

 
\series default
measures the stabilizer 
\begin_inset Formula $\X_{a}\X_{b}\X_{c}\X_{d}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Single Qubit Errors
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Recall that stabilizers codes are initially put into a quiescent state,
 which thereafter remains unchanged except in the presence of errors.
 Consider single-qubit 
\begin_inset Formula $\X$
\end_inset

 spin/bit-flip errors or 
\begin_inset Formula $\Z$
\end_inset

 phase-flip errors.
 Such errors show up in the measurement outcomes of the stabilizers (as
 discussed in section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:The-Stabilizer-Code"

\end_inset

).
 For example
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{K}:=\hat{I}_{a}+\epsilon\Z_{a}
\]

\end_inset

 affecting data qubit 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

.
 This error transform the wavefunction 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
K\psi\rightarrow\psi':=\left(\hat{I}_{a}+\epsilon\Z_{a}\right)\psi\equiv\psi+\epsilon\Z_{a}\psi
\]

\end_inset

What this means is that, upon the next measurement of the neighboring 
\begin_inset Formula $\Z_{a}\Z_{b}\Z_{c}\Z_{d}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\X_{a}\X_{b}\X_{c}\X_{d}$
\end_inset

 stabilizers , 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi'$
\end_inset

 will either be projected
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
I.e., measured to be in.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 back to the original 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi$
\end_inset

 (if we're lucky
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
If 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon$
\end_inset

 is very small, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\epsilon\right|^{2}\ll1$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset

), or it'll be projected to 
\begin_inset Formula $\Z_{a}\psi$
\end_inset

 with probability 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\epsilon\right|^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Obviously in the first case, the measurement outcomes of the stabilizers
 are the same as before the error.
 On the otherhand, in the second case
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\X_{a}\X_{b}\X_{c}\X_{d}\left(\Z_{a}\psi\right)=-\Z_{a}\left(\X_{a}\X_{b}\X_{c}\X_{d}\psi\right)=-X_{abcd}\left(\Z_{a}\psi\right)
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{abcd}$
\end_inset

 is the outcome of the measurement of 
\begin_inset Formula $\X_{a}\X_{b}\X_{c}\X_{d}$
\end_inset

 (i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{abcd}$
\end_inset

 is a real number).
 This proves that 
\begin_inset Formula $\Z_{a}\psi$
\end_inset

 is an eigenstate
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Recall that the definition of an eigenstate 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi$
\end_inset

 for an operator 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $K\psi=k\psi$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 of the 
\begin_inset Formula $\X_{a}\X_{b}\X_{c}\X_{d}$
\end_inset

 stabilizer but with the opposite sign of the original 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi$
\end_inset

.
 
\series bold
Very important
\series default
:
\series bold
 the same argument applies to the other measure-
\begin_inset Formula $\X$
\end_inset

 qubit adjacent to qubit 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

 but such an error has no effect on neighboring measure-
\begin_inset Formula $\Z$
\end_inset

 qubits 
\series default
(since 
\begin_inset Formula $\Z$
\end_inset

s commute).
 Thus, we can detect 
\begin_inset Formula $\Z$
\end_inset

 errors through sign flips in measure-
\begin_inset Formula $\X$
\end_inset

 qubits.
 
\series bold
A little thinking should reveal that the same is true for 
\begin_inset Formula $\X$
\end_inset

 errors
\series default
.
 Correcting such errors could involve simply applying the corresponding
 operators (correct a 
\begin_inset Formula $\Z_{a}$
\end_inset

 phase-flip by applying 
\begin_inset Formula $\Z_{a}$
\end_inset

 again since 
\begin_inset Formula $\Z_{a}^{2}=\hat{I}_{a}$
\end_inset

) but since this could introduce more errors it's usually handled in other
 ways
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Fowler_2012"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Logical Operators
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
How does one perform quantum logic in the surface code? By which we mean,
 given we're continually measuring the measurement qubits (stabilizing),
 how (which) useful operations can we perform on the data qubits without
 interfering with the stabilizers? Put another way: it may seem like process
 of measuring the stabilizers over and over locks the quantum system into
 exactly one logical state by virtue of constraining it to always be in
 an eigenstate of all of the stabilizers.
 That is actually what we implied at the end of section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:The-Stabilizer-Code"

\end_inset


\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

simultaneous and unique eigenstate...
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 But, in fact, the set of stabilizers on an array such as 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "2dlattice-1-1"

\end_inset

 is actually not always complete and so quantum system can have some 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

degrees of freedom
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
What are degrees of freedom? The fancy definition is 
\emph on
the dimension of the configuration space of the system
\emph default
.
 You can think of this quantity, say 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

, as the minimum number of real numbers needed to fully describe the system.
 For example, a tennis ball flying through the air has 6 degrees of freedom:
 3 cartesian coordinates are needed to describe its position, along with
 a velocity vector that has 3 independent components.
 A more interesting example could be something like a rollercoaster car;
 superficially it may look like it has the same number as the tennis ball
 - it is moving through space after all - but since it's locked to the track
 the 3 position coordinates aren't independent amongst each other (and neither
 are the 3 components of the velocity vector).
 Indeed the rollercoaster car is really only traveling in one dimension
 - backwards and forwards along the track - and so it only has 2 degrees
 of freedom (position along the track and velocity).
 More pertinently a single qubit has 2 degrees of freedom: its phase and
 its amplitude or, alternatively the coefficients 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha,\beta$
\end_inset

 if it is interpreted to be in a superposition of 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{0}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{1}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Looking at the array in figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "2dlattice-1-1"

\end_inset

, there are 41 data qubits and 40 measure qubits, so there are 
\begin_inset Formula $2\times41$
\end_inset

 degrees of freedom in the data qubits and 
\begin_inset Formula $2\times40$
\end_inset

 constraints from the stabilizer measurements.
 These 2 extra degrees of freedom (
\begin_inset Formula $2\cdot41-2\cdot40=2$
\end_inset

) means the array can serve as a (single) logical qubit.
 That is to say, we can perform operations on the array that encode useful
 information along those two free dimensions without interfering/disturbing
 the stabilizer measurement scheme/process.
 But how do we find the operators that will allow us to manipulate the additiona
l degrees of freedom in the array, without affecting the stabilizers? One
 observation that points in the right direction is that stabilizer measurements
 by adjacent stabilizers commute because they share a pair of data qubits
 (see figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "2dlattice-1-1-1"

\end_inset

):
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
\left[\X_{a}\X_{b}\X_{c}\X_{d},\Z_{a}\Z_{b}\Z_{e}\Z_{f}\right] & =\left(\X_{a}\Z_{a}\right)\left(\X_{b}\Z_{b}\right)\X_{c}\X_{d}\Z_{e}\Z_{f}-\nonumber \\
 & \qquad\left(\Z_{a}\X_{a}\right)\left(\Z_{b}\X_{b}\right)\X_{c}\X_{d}\Z_{e}\Z_{f}\nonumber \\
 & =\left(\X_{a}\Z_{a}\right)\left(\X_{b}\Z_{b}\right)\X_{c}\X_{d}\Z_{e}\Z_{f}\label{eq:commutestab}\\
 & \qquad-\left(-\Z_{a}\X_{a}\right)\left(-\Z_{b}\X_{b}\right)\X_{c}\X_{d}\Z_{e}\Z_{f}\\
 & =0\nonumber 
\end{align}

\end_inset


