Similarly, products of pairs operators adjacent to the same stabilizer also
 commute with that stabilizer: 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\Z_{a}\Z_{b}\Z_{c}\Z_{d}\left(\X_{a}\X_{b}\right)\psi & =\left(-1\right)^{2}\X_{a}\X_{b}\left(\Z_{a}\Z_{b}\Z_{c}\Z_{d}\right)\psi=Z_{abcd}\X_{a}\X_{b}\psi
\end{align*}

\end_inset

(recall that 
\begin_inset Formula $Z_{abcd}$
\end_inset

is the outcome of the measurement of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Z_{a}\Z_{b}\Z_{c}\Z_{d}$
\end_inset

, i.e., a real number).
 A similar calculation follows for pairs of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Z_{i}$
\end_inset

 adjacent to 
\begin_inset Formula $\X$
\end_inset

 stabilizers.
 This shows that 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

product chains
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 of operators 
\begin_inset Formula $\Z_{i}$
\end_inset

 
\series bold
that stretch across the entire length of the array 
\series default
commute with all 
\begin_inset Formula $\X$
\end_inset

 stabilizers and 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

product chains
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 of 
\begin_inset Formula $\X_{i}$
\end_inset

 commute with all 
\begin_inset Formula $\Z$
\end_inset

 stabilizers.
 Picking two such chains arbitrarily 
\begin_inset Formula $\X_{L}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Z_{L}$
\end_inset

 we have our logical operators (see figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "2dlattice-1-2-1"

\end_inset

), in that they each transform a quiescent state 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi$
\end_inset

 into some new state 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi'$
\end_inset

, which while still an eigenstate of all of the stabilizers, cannot be written
 a product of them
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Each chain is said to 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

manipulate
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 1 degree of freedom by changing 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi\rightarrow\psi'$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 In fact the chains need not even be 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

flat
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 (straight lines); they can be deformed arbitrarily (as long as no crossings
 are introduced).
 For example, consider the pink deformation in figure 
